{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01MKirR6LHRB4RdtHuVnHy8b","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tIterative self-training with reward curation, where a reward model selects the best candidate response at each generation for supervised fine-tuning, is widely expected to erode output diversity through compounding selection pressure. We test this expectation with a controlled factorial experiment spanning 8 configurations, 3 iterative generations, and both Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA) and full fine-tuning. The central finding contradicts the prevailing concern: semantic diversity, measured by Vendi Score over sentence embeddings, remains within $\\pm$1\\% of its initial value across all curated conditions, even at a 6.25\\% retention rate (top-1-of-16) where Vendi Score changes by only $-0.19\\%$. However, this stability masks what we term \\emph{metric-dependent collapse}. Iterative self-training causes substantial format homogenization regardless of curation strategy, with opening unique ratio falling from 0.757 to 0.477 and near-duplicate start rates rising from 37.9\\% to 75.6\\% under matched $K{=}4$ conditions, while embedding-based metrics remain blind to this convergence. We further document a direction reversal: curation suppresses diversity under LoRA ($-3.40$ percentage points relative to a random baseline) but promotes it under full fine-tuning ($+0.63$ pp), a pattern that tracks parameter capacity rather than selection intensity. These results establish that reward curation does not cause semantic collapse in iterative self-training, that diversity audits relying solely on embedding-based measures miss significant structural homogenization, and that conclusions about curation effects depend critically on the training regime under which they are measured."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01Vb4jh89bwG62uBhuit2eMG","session_id":"92cca1d5-ce22-4ffe-a33d-e2fb8f40ddd5","uuid":"1669d99a-dc38-4340-a82f-217e4f394b3d","timestamp":"2026-05-17T14:30:39.757Z"}
