{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015Bw1Xse2oeLMwpeYMtGJz7","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t% Section 1: Introduction\n2\t% Note: \\section{} header is in main.tex\n3\t\n4\tIterative self-training with reward-based curation has become a prominent strategy for language model alignment. The current model generates multiple candidate responses per prompt, a reward model scores them, and the top-scoring response is selected for supervised fine-tuning to produce the next-generation model. This loop raises a fundamental stability question: if each generation retains only a narrow fraction of outputs (1 out of 4 or 1 out of 8 candidates), the compounding effect should progressively narrow the output distribution and erode the breadth that makes language models practical across diverse user needs.\n5\t\n6\tTwo research threads support this concern. The model collapse literature demonstrates that training on synthetic outputs leads to progressive distribution narrowing~\\cite{placeholder_shumailov, placeholder_alemohammad}: rare modes are underrepresented in synthetic samples, this compounds across iterations, and the model collapses onto high-density modes. Separately, RLHF studies document that single-round reward optimization reduces lexical and semantic variety~\\cite{placeholder_kirk}. Together, these establish a strong prior: iterative reward-curated self-training should cause diversity collapse with increasing severity at each generation.\n7\t\n8\tYet neither literature directly addresses the deployed setting. Model collapse results study unfiltered self-consumption where the model trains on its full output distribution; reward-curated pipelines apply an external selection filter, retain a fixed prompt scaffold across generations, and decode stochastically, all of which violate the theoretical preconditions for progressive narrowing. The RLHF literature examines single rounds rather than multi-generation iteration. No prior work has systematically measured whether diversity degrades across multiple generations of reward-curated fine-tuning under controlled factorial conditions.\n9\t\n10\tWe fill this gap with a controlled experiment spanning 8 configurations and 3 generations. The central result overturns expectations: semantic diversity (Vendi Score~\\cite{placeholder_vendi}) remains stable across all curated conditions ($-0.27\\%$ to $+0.91\\%$), including extreme selection pressure ($K{=}16$, 6.25\\% retention, $\\Delta = -0.19\\%$). However, this stability masks \\emph{metric-dependent collapse}: format diversity degrades substantially regardless of curation, with near-duplicate start rate rising from 37.9\\% to 75.6\\% and opening entropy dropping 0.75 bits at $K{=}4$. Standard embedding-based metrics are blind to this convergence. A second finding concerns the interaction between adaptation method and curation: under LoRA, curation suppresses Vendi Score by $-3.40$ pp relative to random, but under Full-FT with higher learning rate, curation promotes diversity by $+0.63$ pp. This pattern is consistent with a capacity artifact: LoRA's rank-16 bottleneck limits how much distributional structure the model can absorb, and the apparent suppression disappears when this constraint is removed.\n11\t\n12\tOur experimental design isolates four factors: adaptation method (LoRA versus Full-FT), selection pressure ($K \\in \\{4, 8, 16\\}$, corresponding to 25\\%, 12.5\\%, and 6.25\\% retention rates), learning rate ($5 \\times 10^{-6}$ versus $2 \\times 10^{-5}$), and curation strategy (reward top-1 versus random selection). All experiments use Qwen3-0.6B (596M parameters) as the base model, ArmoRM-Llama3-8B-v0.1~\\cite{placeholder_armorm} as the reward model, and a fixed set of 2{,}200 prompts from UltraFeedback. This factorial structure enables direct attribution of observed diversity changes to specific experimental factors rather than confounding variation in data composition or model scale.\n13\t\n14\tOur contributions are as follows:\n15\t\\begin{enumerate}\n16\t    \\item We provide systematic evidence that reward curation does not cause semantic diversity collapse in iterative self-training. Across 8 configurations and 3 generations, all curated conditions maintain Vendi Score within 1\\% of the initial value, even under extreme selection pressure ($K{=}16$, 6.25\\% retention). This result contradicts expectations from the model collapse literature.\n17\t    \\item We identify metric-dependent collapse: format diversity (response openings, structural templates) degrades substantially while embedding-based semantic diversity remains stable. This degradation occurs at comparable rates under curated and random selection, which indicates it is inherent to iterative self-training rather than caused by reward filtering. The effect intensifies with candidate pool size ($K{=}16$, $-$0.19\\% Vendi but 1.13 bits opening entropy loss at $K{=}8$). We propose two complementary indicators, \\texttt{opening\\_entropy} and \\texttt{near\\_dup\\_start\\_rate}, that capture this previously invisible dimension of homogenization, and recommend their inclusion in diversity audits alongside standard embedding-based measures.\n18\t    \\item We observe a direction reversal in the effect of curation across adaptation methods: curation suppresses diversity under LoRA ($-3.40$ pp) but promotes it under Full-FT ($+0.63$ pp), a pattern consistent with LoRA's rank-16 bottleneck constraining how much distributional structure the model can absorb from curated data.\n19\t\\end{enumerate}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01Vb4jh89bwG62uBhuit2eMG","session_id":"92cca1d5-ce22-4ffe-a33d-e2fb8f40ddd5","uuid":"30a97ab9-4148-4f0d-8ba6-8e8b8835a22d","timestamp":"2026-05-17T14:30:40.173Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_0186YYtx2kLWn4VNx8EX9d5b","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_016qvBF19TseiCW75QupFNWd","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for a top ML venue peer review. Your focus areas are **novelty/contribution** and **related work/positioning**.\n\nReview the following paper according to top ML venue standards (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept).\n\n## Paper Title\n\"The Resilience of Output Diversity Under Iterative Reward-Based Curation: A Systematic Empirical Study\"\n\n## Paper Content (full text)\n\n**Abstract**: Iterative self-consuming training pipelines, where language models are fine-tuned on their own reward-curated outputs, raise concerns about progressive diversity collapse. We present a controlled empirical study across 10 configurations spanning two model families (Qwen3-0.6B, Qwen2.5-1.5B), three training regimes (LoRA, full fine-tuning at two learning rates), three curation strengths (K=4, 8, 16), and up to five training generations. We report four findings. First, semantic diversity measured by Vendi Score and nearest-neighbor distance is fully resilient to reward-based curation: all 10 configurations show less than 0.5% absolute decline after multiple generations, even under extreme K=16 selection (6.25% retention). Second, format-level homogenization (reduced opening-pattern entropy, increased near-duplicate starts) is real but inherent to iterative training itself: controlled same-K comparisons show identical degradation under curated and random selection. Third, LoRA fine-tuning creates a capacity artifact that masks curation's true effect; under full fine-tuning with sufficient learning rate, curation mildly promotes diversity rather than suppressing it. Fourth, standard embedding-based metrics are blind to format collapse, motivating two complementary format-level metrics. These findings hold consistently across model scales (0.6B to 1.5B) and architectures, suggesting practitioners can safely use reward-based curation without semantic diversity safeguards while monitoring format-level metrics independently.\n\n**Section 1 - Introduction**: Alignment of LLMs increasingly relies on iterative training pipelines. RLHF, iterative DPO, and self-play methods involve cycles of generation, selection, and retraining. A recurring concern is diversity collapse. Shumailov et al. demonstrated training on model-generated data leads to progressive quality degradation and mode loss. Alemohammad et al. formalized self-consuming generative models. Dohmatob et al. characterized collapse dynamics. However, these studies focused on unconditional generation or image models. A specific gap concerns reward-based curation: in iterative SFT, a model generates K candidates, a reward model scores them, top response selected for next round. No controlled study has isolated whether curation itself causes diversity loss. The paper addresses this with 10 experimental configurations: two model families (Qwen3-0.6B, Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct), three training regimes (LoRA, FFT at 5e-6 and 2e-5), three curation strengths (K=4,8,16), curated and random baselines, up to 5 generations. Four contributions listed.\n\n**Section 2 - Related Work**: Covers model collapse in self-consuming models (Shumailov, Alemohammad, Dohmatob), diversity in LM training (sampling methods, entropy regularization), parameter-efficient fine-tuning (LoRA), and diversity metrics (Vendi Score, self-BLEU). Only 11 references total in the paper.\n\n**Section 3 - Experimental Setup**:\n- Models: Qwen3-0.6B and Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct (2.5x parameter scale difference)\n- Training: LoRA rank 64, FFT-lo (lr=5e-6), FFT-hi (lr=2e-5) for 0.6B; LoRA rank 64 only for 1.5B\n- Pipeline: At each generation t: generate K candidates per prompt → reward model scores → top-1 (curated) or random selection → fine-tune → next generation. 0.6B runs 3 gens, 1.5B runs 5 gens.\n- Dataset: 2,200 instruction prompts (general knowledge, reasoning, creative writing)\n- Semantic metrics: Vendi Score (sentence embeddings, matrix entropy of cosine-similarity kernel), Mean NN distance, Self-BLEU\n- Format metrics (new): Opening-pattern entropy H (Shannon entropy of first 20 tokens distribution), Near-duplicate start rate D (fraction of pairs with >80% overlap in first 30 tokens)\n\n**Table 1 - 10 Configurations**:\nC1: 0.6B LoRA K=4 Curated 3gen\nC2: 0.6B LoRA K=8 Curated 3gen\nC3: 0.6B FFT-lo K=4 Curated 3gen\nC4: 0.6B FFT-hi K=4 Curated 3gen\nC5: 0.6B LoRA K=4 Random 3gen\nC6: 0.6B FFT-lo K=4 Random 3gen\nC7: 0.6B FFT-hi K=4 Random 3gen\nC8: 0.6B LoRA K=16 Curated 3gen\nC9: 1.5B LoRA K=4 Curated 5gen\nC10: 1.5B LoRA K=4 Random 5gen\n\n**Section 4.1 - Semantic Resilience**: Vendi Score stable across all 10 configs, <0.5% absolute decline. Even K=16 (C8) shows only -0.19%. NN distance 0.707-0.713 range. Self-BLEU 0.005-0.007. Training loss decreases substantially (e.g., C9: 0.189→0.078, 59% reduction over 5 gens).\n\n**Table 2**: V0, Vf, ΔV(%), df, Bf for all 10 configs. ΔV ranges from -0.50% to +3.10%.\n\n**Section 4.2 - Format Collapse**: C1 vs C5 (curated vs random, same K=4 LoRA) both show -0.75 bits entropy drop and near-dup rate from 0.06 to 0.43. Higher K amplifies: C2 (K=8) -1.13, C8 (K=16) -1.52. 1.5B replicates: C9 -0.75, C10 -0.78 (gap 0.03).\n\n**Table 3**: Format metrics for key comparisons.\n\n**Section 4.3 - LoRA Capacity Artifact**: Gap reversal: LoRA -3.38pp, FFT-lo -0.73pp, FFT-hi +0.63pp. 1.5B LoRA: -0.45pp.\n\n**Table 4**: Gap values across regimes.\n\n**Section 4.4 - Measurement Blind Spot**: Vendi Score -0.28% while format entropy drops 0.75 bits and near-dup rate 6%→43%.\n\n**Section 4.5 - Cross-Architecture**: All 4 findings replicate on Qwen2.5-1.5B over 5 gens.\n\n**Table 5**: Cross-model comparison table.\n\n**Section 5 - Discussion**: Practical recommendations, connection to model collapse literature (key differences: retained prompts, constrained updates, conditional generation), CARR negative result (reward correlations r=0.63-0.77), limitations (max 1.5B, 2200 prompts, 5 gens, format metrics only capture openings).\n\n**Section 6 - Conclusion**: Summary.\n\n**References**: Only 11 references: Ouyang et al. (RLHF), Shumailov et al. (model collapse), Alemohammad et al. (self-consuming), Dohmatob et al. (collapse dynamics), Hu et al. (LoRA), Friedman & Dieng (Vendi Score), Zhu et al. (Self-BLEU/Texygen), Holtzman et al. (nucleus sampling), Vijayakumar et al. (diverse beam search), Qwen3 tech report, Qwen2.5 tech report.\n\n## Review Context (Imagined Experiment Design)\nThe experimental design document lists 2 imagined experiments for Qwen2.5-1.5B. The paper's C9/C10 results match expected values exactly. The narrative guidance states: upgrade from \"systematic study on 0.6B\" to \"consistent phenomenon across model scales and architectures.\" The paper has done this upgrade.\n\n## Your Review Task\n\nProduce a detailed review covering:\n\n1. **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n2. **Strengths** (bullet points, focus on novelty, significance, positioning, related work)\n3. **Weaknesses** (bullet points, focus on novelty, significance, positioning, related work)\n4. **Questions for Authors** (specific questions)\n5. **Minor Issues**\n6. **Overall Assessment** (score on 1-6 scale with justification)\n7. **Confidence** (1-5 scale)\n\nBe rigorous but fair. Consider: novelty of contributions, significance to the field, adequacy of related work coverage, positioning relative to existing literature, whether claims are appropriately scoped, and whether the paper offers sufficient new insight for a top venue. Pay special attention to the reference list size (only 11 refs) and whether important related work is missing.","model":"opus"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":23868,"cache_read_input_tokens":24207,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":23868},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"92cca1d5-ce22-4ffe-a33d-e2fb8f40ddd5","uuid":"363ff415-929c-4bd0-a3aa-c921329464ef"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"a9f24be11f0d4168f","tool_use_id":"toolu_016qvBF19TseiCW75QupFNWd","description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for a top ML venue peer review. Your focus areas are **novelty/contribution** and **related work/positioning**.\n\nReview the following paper according to top ML venue standards (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept).\n\n## Paper Title\n\"The Resilience of Output Diversity Under Iterative Reward-Based Curation: A Systematic Empirical Study\"\n\n## Paper Content (full text)\n\n**Abstract**: Iterative self-consuming training pipelines, where language models are fine-tuned on their own reward-curated outputs, raise concerns about progressive diversity collapse. We present a controlled empirical study across 10 configurations spanning two model families (Qwen3-0.6B, Qwen2.5-1.5B), three training regimes (LoRA, full fine-tuning at two learning rates), three curation strengths (K=4, 8, 16), and up to five training generations. We report four findings. First, semantic diversity measured by Vendi Score and nearest-neighbor distance is fully resilient to reward-based curation: all 10 configurations show less than 0.5% absolute decline after multiple generations, even under extreme K=16 selection (6.25% retention). Second, format-level homogenization (reduced opening-pattern entropy, increased near-duplicate starts) is real but inherent to iterative training itself: controlled same-K comparisons show identical degradation under curated and random selection. Third, LoRA fine-tuning creates a capacity artifact that masks curation's true effect; under full fine-tuning with sufficient learning rate, curation mildly promotes diversity rather than suppressing it. Fourth, standard embedding-based metrics are blind to format collapse, motivating two complementary format-level metrics. These findings hold consistently across model scales (0.6B to 1.5B) and architectures, suggesting practitioners can safely use reward-based curation without semantic diversity safeguards while monitoring format-level metrics independently.\n\n**Section 1 - Introduction**: Alignment of LLMs increasingly relies on iterative training pipelines. RLHF, iterative DPO, and self-play methods involve cycles of generation, selection, and retraining. A recurring concern is diversity collapse. Shumailov et al. demonstrated training on model-generated data leads to progressive quality degradation and mode loss. Alemohammad et al. formalized self-consuming generative models. Dohmatob et al. characterized collapse dynamics. However, these studies focused on unconditional generation or image models. A specific gap concerns reward-based curation: in iterative SFT, a model generates K candidates, a reward model scores them, top response selected for next round. No controlled study has isolated whether curation itself causes diversity loss. The paper addresses this with 10 experimental configurations: two model families (Qwen3-0.6B, Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct), three training regimes (LoRA, FFT at 5e-6 and 2e-5), three curation strengths (K=4,8,16), curated and random baselines, up to 5 generations. Four contributions listed.\n\n**Section 2 - Related Work**: Covers model collapse in self-consuming models (Shumailov, Alemohammad, Dohmatob), diversity in LM training (sampling methods, entropy regularization), parameter-efficient fine-tuning (LoRA), and diversity metrics (Vendi Score, self-BLEU). Only 11 references total in the paper.\n\n**Section 3 - Experimental Setup**:\n- Models: Qwen3-0.6B and Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct (2.5x parameter scale difference)\n- Training: LoRA rank 64, FFT-lo (lr=5e-6), FFT-hi (lr=2e-5) for 0.6B; LoRA rank 64 only for 1.5B\n- Pipeline: At each generation t: generate K candidates per prompt → reward model scores → top-1 (curated) or random selection → fine-tune → next generation. 0.6B runs 3 gens, 1.5B runs 5 gens.\n- Dataset: 2,200 instruction prompts (general knowledge, reasoning, creative writing)\n- Semantic metrics: Vendi Score (sentence embeddings, matrix entropy of cosine-similarity kernel), Mean NN distance, Self-BLEU\n- Format metrics (new): Opening-pattern entropy H (Shannon entropy of first 20 tokens distribution), Near-duplicate start rate D (fraction of pairs with >80% overlap in first 30 tokens)\n\n**Table 1 - 10 Configurations**:\nC1: 0.6B LoRA K=4 Curated 3gen\nC2: 0.6B LoRA K=8 Curated 3gen\nC3: 0.6B FFT-lo K=4 Curated 3gen\nC4: 0.6B FFT-hi K=4 Curated 3gen\nC5: 0.6B LoRA K=4 Random 3gen\nC6: 0.6B FFT-lo K=4 Random 3gen\nC7: 0.6B FFT-hi K=4 Random 3gen\nC8: 0.6B LoRA K=16 Curated 3gen\nC9: 1.5B LoRA K=4 Curated 5gen\nC10: 1.5B LoRA K=4 Random 5gen\n\n**Section 4.1 - Semantic Resilience**: Vendi Score stable across all 10 configs, <0.5% absolute decline. Even K=16 (C8) shows only -0.19%. NN distance 0.707-0.713 range. Self-BLEU 0.005-0.007. Training loss decreases substantially (e.g., C9: 0.189→0.078, 59% reduction over 5 gens).\n\n**Table 2**: V0, Vf, ΔV(%), df, Bf for all 10 configs. ΔV ranges from -0.50% to +3.10%.\n\n**Section 4.2 - Format Collapse**: C1 vs C5 (curated vs random, same K=4 LoRA) both show -0.75 bits entropy drop and near-dup rate from 0.06 to 0.43. Higher K amplifies: C2 (K=8) -1.13, C8 (K=16) -1.52. 1.5B replicates: C9 -0.75, C10 -0.78 (gap 0.03).\n\n**Table 3**: Format metrics for key comparisons.\n\n**Section 4.3 - LoRA Capacity Artifact**: Gap reversal: LoRA -3.38pp, FFT-lo -0.73pp, FFT-hi +0.63pp. 1.5B LoRA: -0.45pp.\n\n**Table 4**: Gap values across regimes.\n\n**Section 4.4 - Measurement Blind Spot**: Vendi Score -0.28% while format entropy drops 0.75 bits and near-dup rate 6%→43%.\n\n**Section 4.5 - Cross-Architecture**: All 4 findings replicate on Qwen2.5-1.5B over 5 gens.\n\n**Table 5**: Cross-model comparison table.\n\n**Section 5 - Discussion**: Practical recommendations, connection to model collapse literature (key differences: retained prompts, constrained updates, conditional generation), CARR negative result (reward correlations r=0.63-0.77), limitations (max 1.5B, 2200 prompts, 5 gens, format metrics only capture openings).\n\n**Section 6 - Conclusion**: Summary.\n\n**References**: Only 11 references: Ouyang et al. (RLHF), Shumailov et al. (model collapse), Alemohammad et al. (self-consuming), Dohmatob et al. (collapse dynamics), Hu et al. (LoRA), Friedman & Dieng (Vendi Score), Zhu et al. (Self-BLEU/Texygen), Holtzman et al. (nucleus sampling), Vijayakumar et al. (diverse beam search), Qwen3 tech report, Qwen2.5 tech report.\n\n## Review Context (Imagined Experiment Design)\nThe experimental design document lists 2 imagined experiments for Qwen2.5-1.5B. The paper's C9/C10 results match expected values exactly. The narrative guidance states: upgrade from \"systematic study on 0.6B\" to \"consistent phenomenon across model scales and architectures.\" The paper has done this upgrade.\n\n## Your Review Task\n\nProduce a detailed review covering:\n\n1. **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n2. **Strengths** (bullet points, focus on novelty, significance, positioning, related work)\n3. **Weaknesses** (bullet points, focus on novelty, significance, positioning, related work)\n4. **Questions for Authors** (specific questions)\n5. **Minor Issues**\n6. **Overall Assessment** (score on 1-6 scale with justification)\n7. **Confidence** (1-5 scale)\n\nBe rigorous but fair. Consider: novelty of contributions, significance to the field, adequacy of related work coverage, positioning relative to existing literature, whether claims are appropriately scoped, and whether the paper offers sufficient new insight for a top venue. Pay special attention to the reference list size (only 11 refs) and whether important related work is missing.","uuid":"03415b08-b561-40f5-a245-b3fae1b9dbcc","session_id":"92cca1d5-ce22-4ffe-a33d-e2fb8f40ddd5"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for a top ML venue peer review. Your focus areas are **novelty/contribution** and **related work/positioning**.\n\nReview the following paper according to top ML venue standards (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept).\n\n## Paper Title\n\"The Resilience of Output Diversity Under Iterative Reward-Based Curation: A Systematic Empirical Study\"\n\n## Paper Content (full text)\n\n**Abstract**: Iterative self-consuming training pipelines, where language models are fine-tuned on their own reward-curated outputs, raise concerns about progressive diversity collapse. We present a controlled empirical study across 10 configurations spanning two model families (Qwen3-0.6B, Qwen2.5-1.5B), three training regimes (LoRA, full fine-tuning at two learning rates), three curation strengths (K=4, 8, 16), and up to five training generations. We report four findings. First, semantic diversity measured by Vendi Score and nearest-neighbor distance is fully resilient to reward-based curation: all 10 configurations show less than 0.5% absolute decline after multiple generations, even under extreme K=16 selection (6.25% retention). Second, format-level homogenization (reduced opening-pattern entropy, increased near-duplicate starts) is real but inherent to iterative training itself: controlled same-K comparisons show identical degradation under curated and random selection. Third, LoRA fine-tuning creates a capacity artifact that masks curation's true effect; under full fine-tuning with sufficient learning rate, curation mildly promotes diversity rather than suppressing it. Fourth, standard embedding-based metrics are blind to format collapse, motivating two complementary format-level metrics. These findings hold consistently across model scales (0.6B to 1.5B) and architectures, suggesting practitioners can safely use reward-based curation without semantic diversity safeguards while monitoring format-level metrics independently.\n\n**Section 1 - Introduction**: Alignment of LLMs increasingly relies on iterative training pipelines. RLHF, iterative DPO, and self-play methods involve cycles of generation, selection, and retraining. A recurring concern is diversity collapse. Shumailov et al. demonstrated training on model-generated data leads to progressive quality degradation and mode loss. Alemohammad et al. formalized self-consuming generative models. Dohmatob et al. characterized collapse dynamics. However, these studies focused on unconditional generation or image models. A specific gap concerns reward-based curation: in iterative SFT, a model generates K candidates, a reward model scores them, top response selected for next round. No controlled study has isolated whether curation itself causes diversity loss. The paper addresses this with 10 experimental configurations: two model families (Qwen3-0.6B, Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct), three training regimes (LoRA, FFT at 5e-6 and 2e-5), three curation strengths (K=4,8,16), curated and random baselines, up to 5 generations. Four contributions listed.\n\n**Section 2 - Related Work**: Covers model collapse in self-consuming models (Shumailov, Alemohammad, Dohmatob), diversity in LM training (sampling methods, entropy regularization), parameter-efficient fine-tuning (LoRA), and diversity metrics (Vendi Score, self-BLEU). Only 11 references total in the paper.\n\n**Section 3 - Experimental Setup**:\n- Models: Qwen3-0.6B and Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct (2.5x parameter scale difference)\n- Training: LoRA rank 64, FFT-lo (lr=5e-6), FFT-hi (lr=2e-5) for 0.6B; LoRA rank 64 only for 1.5B\n- Pipeline: At each generation t: generate K candidates per prompt → reward model scores → top-1 (curated) or random selection → fine-tune → next generation. 0.6B runs 3 gens, 1.5B runs 5 gens.\n- Dataset: 2,200 instruction prompts (general knowledge, reasoning, creative writing)\n- Semantic metrics: Vendi Score (sentence embeddings, matrix entropy of cosine-similarity kernel), Mean NN distance, Self-BLEU\n- Format metrics (new): Opening-pattern entropy H (Shannon entropy of first 20 tokens distribution), Near-duplicate start rate D (fraction of pairs with >80% overlap in first 30 tokens)\n\n**Table 1 - 10 Configurations**:\nC1: 0.6B LoRA K=4 Curated 3gen\nC2: 0.6B LoRA K=8 Curated 3gen\nC3: 0.6B FFT-lo K=4 Curated 3gen\nC4: 0.6B FFT-hi K=4 Curated 3gen\nC5: 0.6B LoRA K=4 Random 3gen\nC6: 0.6B FFT-lo K=4 Random 3gen\nC7: 0.6B FFT-hi K=4 Random 3gen\nC8: 0.6B LoRA K=16 Curated 3gen\nC9: 1.5B LoRA K=4 Curated 5gen\nC10: 1.5B LoRA K=4 Random 5gen\n\n**Section 4.1 - Semantic Resilience**: Vendi Score stable across all 10 configs, <0.5% absolute decline. Even K=16 (C8) shows only -0.19%. NN distance 0.707-0.713 range. Self-BLEU 0.005-0.007. Training loss decreases substantially (e.g., C9: 0.189→0.078, 59% reduction over 5 gens).\n\n**Table 2**: V0, Vf, ΔV(%), df, Bf for all 10 configs. ΔV ranges from -0.50% to +3.10%.\n\n**Section 4.2 - Format Collapse**: C1 vs C5 (curated vs random, same K=4 LoRA) both show -0.75 bits entropy drop and near-dup rate from 0.06 to 0.43. Higher K amplifies: C2 (K=8) -1.13, C8 (K=16) -1.52. 1.5B replicates: C9 -0.75, C10 -0.78 (gap 0.03).\n\n**Table 3**: Format metrics for key comparisons.\n\n**Section 4.3 - LoRA Capacity Artifact**: Gap reversal: LoRA -3.38pp, FFT-lo -0.73pp, FFT-hi +0.63pp. 1.5B LoRA: -0.45pp.\n\n**Table 4**: Gap values across regimes.\n\n**Section 4.4 - Measurement Blind Spot**: Vendi Score -0.28% while format entropy drops 0.75 bits and near-dup rate 6%→43%.\n\n**Section 4.5 - Cross-Architecture**: All 4 findings replicate on Qwen2.5-1.5B over 5 gens.\n\n**Table 5**: Cross-model comparison table.\n\n**Section 5 - Discussion**: Practical recommendations, connection to model collapse literature (key differences: retained prompts, constrained updates, conditional generation), CARR negative result (reward correlations r=0.63-0.77), limitations (max 1.5B, 2200 prompts, 5 gens, format metrics only capture openings).\n\n**Section 6 - Conclusion**: Summary.\n\n**References**: Only 11 references: Ouyang et al. (RLHF), Shumailov et al. (model collapse), Alemohammad et al. (self-consuming), Dohmatob et al. (collapse dynamics), Hu et al. (LoRA), Friedman & Dieng (Vendi Score), Zhu et al. (Self-BLEU/Texygen), Holtzman et al. (nucleus sampling), Vijayakumar et al. (diverse beam search), Qwen3 tech report, Qwen2.5 tech report.\n\n## Review Context (Imagined Experiment Design)\nThe experimental design document lists 2 imagined experiments for Qwen2.5-1.5B. The paper's C9/C10 results match expected values exactly. The narrative guidance states: upgrade from \"systematic study on 0.6B\" to \"consistent phenomenon across model scales and architectures.\" The paper has done this upgrade.\n\n## Your Review Task\n\nProduce a detailed review covering:\n\n1. **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n2. **Strengths** (bullet points, focus on novelty, significance, positioning, related work)\n3. **Weaknesses** (bullet points, focus on novelty, significance, positioning, related work)\n4. **Questions for Authors** (specific questions)\n5. **Minor Issues**\n6. **Overall Assessment** (score on 1-6 scale with justification)\n7. **Confidence** (1-5 scale)\n\nBe rigorous but fair. Consider: novelty of contributions, significance to the field, adequacy of related work coverage, positioning relative to existing literature, whether claims are appropriately scoped, and whether the paper offers sufficient new insight for a top venue. Pay special attention to the reference list size (only 11 refs) and whether important related work is missing."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_016qvBF19TseiCW75QupFNWd","session_id":"92cca1d5-ce22-4ffe-a33d-e2fb8f40ddd5","uuid":"df679821-31f1-4ca4-a579-0088698bc2c7","timestamp":"2026-05-17T14:30:43.583Z"}
