{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01RwmbH2HugSuXyyczPYWF3Q","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t% Limitations section content below\n2\t% Note: \\section{} header is in main.tex\n3\t\n4\tOur findings are subject to several scope constraints that bound their generalizability.\n5\t\n6\t\\paragraph{Model Scale.}\n7\tAll experiments use Qwen3-0.6B (596M parameters). Larger models produce higher-quality candidates with greater score variance, which could amplify curation effects in either direction: stronger selection pressure from wider score gaps, or greater resilience from richer internal representations. Whether the semantic stability we observe holds at the 7B or 70B scale remains an open question.\n8\t\n9\t\\paragraph{Generation Depth.}\n10\tWe evaluate three generations of iterative self-training. The model collapse literature typically requires more iterations before distributional narrowing becomes severe \\cite{placeholder_shumailov}. Our results establish that reward curation is safe over three generations, but they do not guarantee stability at generation ten or beyond. Subtle compounding effects below our measurement threshold could accumulate over longer horizons.\n11\t\n12\t\\paragraph{Domain Coverage.}\n13\tThe UltraFeedback prompt set (2,200 instructions) covers general instruction-following across diverse topics. Narrow domains such as code generation or mathematical reasoning impose tighter output constraints and may exhibit different collapse dynamics. In particular, domains where correct answers cluster tightly in embedding space could reduce the protective effect of prompt diversity.\n14\t\n15\t\\paragraph{Single Reward Model.}\n16\tWe use ArmoRM-Llama3-8B-v0.1 as the sole reward signal throughout all experiments. Different reward models encode different biases: some may penalize structural variety more aggressively, others may exhibit sharper score distributions that strengthen curation pressure. The specific format collapse patterns we document (opening template convergence, near-duplicate starts) reflect ArmoRM's preferences and may not generalize to all reward models.\n17\t\n18\t\\paragraph{Format Metric Granularity.}\n19\tOpening entropy and near-duplicate start rate capture homogenization in the first 10 tokens and 50 characters of each response, respectively. These metrics are blind to structural convergence in middle sections, concluding paragraphs, or organizational patterns such as list usage and paragraph structure. A complete characterization of format collapse would require finer-grained positional analysis that our current metrics do not provide."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01Vb4jh89bwG62uBhuit2eMG","session_id":"92cca1d5-ce22-4ffe-a33d-e2fb8f40ddd5","uuid":"cfd12e72-df3b-4276-b6b4-1f2dda3c1454","timestamp":"2026-05-17T14:30:52.405Z"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01D6XigzG1CCBXP8yjc1wfhg","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t% Appendix\n2\t% Note: \\appendix command is in main.tex\n3\t\n4\t\\section{Training Details}\n5\t\\label{app:training_details}\n6\t\n7\tTable~\\ref{tab:hyperparams} lists the complete hyperparameter settings for all experimental conditions.\n8\t\n9\t\\begin{table}[h]\n10\t\\centering\n11\t\\small\n12\t\\caption{Hyperparameters for all experimental conditions.}\n13\t\\label{tab:hyperparams}\n14\t\\resizebox{\\columnwidth}{!}{%\n15\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}lcc@{}}\n16\t\\toprule\n17\tParameter & LoRA & Full-FT \\\\\n18\t\\midrule\n19\tBase model & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{Qwen3-0.6B} \\\\\n20\tReward model & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{ArmoRM-Llama3-8B-v0.1} \\\\\n21\tPrompts (train / eval) & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{2000 / 200} \\\\\n22\tGenerations & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{3} \\\\\n23\tTemperature & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{0.7} \\\\\n24\tMax new tokens & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{1024} \\\\\n25\tEpochs per generation & 2 & 2 (C4, C8: 3) \\\\\n26\tPer-device batch size & 4 & 1 \\\\\n27\tGradient accumulation & 4 & 8 \\\\\n28\tOptimizer & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{AdamW} \\\\\n29\tWeight decay & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{0.01} \\\\\n30\tWarmup ratio & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{0.1} \\\\\n31\tPrecision & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{bf16} \\\\\n32\tLearning rate & $2 \\times 10^{-4}$ & $5 \\times 10^{-6}$ / $2 \\times 10^{-5}$ \\\\\n33\tLoRA rank & 16 & -- \\\\\n34\tLoRA alpha & 32 & -- \\\\\n35\tLoRA target & \\parbox{3.2cm}{\\raggedright q/k/v/o\\_proj,\\\\gate/up/down\\_proj} & -- \\\\\n36\tGradient checkpointing & No & Yes \\\\\n37\t\\bottomrule\n38\t\\end{tabular}}\n39\t\\end{table}\n40\t\n41\t\\section{LoRA as Capacity Bottleneck}\n42\t\\label{app:lora_bottleneck}\n43\t\n44\tThe similarity of diversity outcomes between $K{=}4$ and $K{=}8$ LoRA conditions (C1: $-$0.27\\%, C2: $+$0.04\\%) despite doubled selection pressure suggests that parameter capacity, not curation intensity, is the binding constraint under LoRA. If curation were the primary driver of diversity change, doubling the candidate pool from 4 to 8 (reducing the retention rate from 25\\% to 12.5\\%) should produce measurably greater diversity suppression. Instead, both conditions converge to nearly identical Gen~2 Vendi Scores (107.25 for both C1 and C2). This convergence is consistent with a capacity-limited regime in which the adapter cannot express the differences between curated and non-curated training signals.\n45\t\n46\tThe rank-16 LoRA adapter modifies only 1.67\\% of model parameters. All learned changes, whether driven by reward-curated data or random data, must be expressed within this low-dimensional subspace. Training loss provides direct evidence of this constraint: C1 (curated, $K{=}4$) reaches a final loss of 0.166, C2 (curated, $K{=}8$) reaches 0.168, and C5 (random) reaches 0.152. The 0.014 gap between curated and random final loss is small relative to the 0.240 reduction from Gen~0 to Gen~2 within each condition (Table~\\ref{tab:training_loss}). The adapter absorbs most of the training signal's structure regardless of whether that signal comes from curated or random selection; the residual difference in loss translates to a negligible difference in diversity outcomes.\n47\t\n48\tThis interpretation reframes the 3.40~pp curation gap reported in Section~\\ref{sec:direction_reversal}. The gap does not reflect curation actively suppressing diversity. Rather, random data's uncorrelated gradients happen to push the LoRA subspace in directions that slightly increase output diversity, while curated data's correlated gradients (consistent reward-preferred patterns) do not. Under full fine-tuning, where the capacity constraint is removed, this asymmetry disappears and eventually reverses (Section~\\ref{sec:direction_reversal}). The practical implication is that LoRA-based studies of reward curation effects should be interpreted cautiously. Observed diversity changes under parameter-efficient fine-tuning may reflect adapter capacity limits rather than properties of the curation strategy itself.\n49\t\n50\t\\section{Per-Generation Metrics}\n51\t\\label{app:per_gen_metrics}\n52\t\n53\tTable~\\ref{tab:per_gen_metrics} reports four diversity and fluency metrics for every condition at each self-training generation.\n54\tVendi Score measures semantic diversity via matrix-rank entropy over embedding similarities; higher values indicate a more diverse output distribution.\n55\tSelf-BLEU averages pairwise BLEU between generated responses; lower values indicate less repetition.\n56\tPerplexity (PPL) is computed with a held-out language model; stable values indicate no fluency degradation.\n57\tNearest-neighbor distance (NND) is the mean cosine distance to the closest other response in embedding space; higher values reflect greater spread.\n58\t\n59\t\\begin{table*}[t]\n60\t\\centering\n61\t\\small\n62\t\\caption{Per-generation metrics for all 8 experimental conditions across 3 self-training generations. Curated conditions (C1--C4, C8) show stable Vendi Score ($\\pm$1\\%), low Self-BLEU, and consistent NND, indicating no semantic collapse. Random baselines (C5--C7) exhibit comparable or larger metric drift despite the absence of reward selection. C7 metrics were recorded from a separate analysis pass.}\n63\t\\label{tab:per_gen_metrics}\n64\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}llcccc@{}}\n65\t\\toprule\n66\tCondition & Gen & Vendi Score & Self-BLEU ($\\downarrow$) & PPL & NND \\\\\n67\t\\midrule\n68\t\\multicolumn{6}{l}{\\textit{Curated conditions}} \\\\\n69\t\\midrule\n70\tC1: LoRA, $K{=}4$\n71\t  & 0 & 107.54 & 0.0059 & 10.60 & 0.7088 \\\\\n72\t  & 1 & 106.73 & 0.0091 &  9.73 & 0.7065 \\\\\n73\t  & 2 & 107.25 & 0.0062 &  9.72 & 0.7054 \\\\\n74\t\\midrule\n75\tC2: LoRA, $K{=}8$\n76\t  & 0 & 107.21 & 0.0057 &  9.40 & 0.7097 \\\\\n77\t  & 1 & 107.26 & 0.0024 &  9.18 & 0.7086 \\\\\n78\t  & 2 & 107.25 & 0.0064 & 10.20 & 0.7095 \\\\\n79\t\\midrule\n80\tC3: Full-FT, 5e-6\n81\t  & 0 & 107.10 & 0.0038 &  9.10 & 0.7073 \\\\\n82\t  & 1 & 107.61 & 0.0060 &  8.66 & 0.7091 \\\\\n83\t  & 2 & 108.07 & 0.0044 &  9.21 & 0.7108 \\\\\n84\t\\midrule\n85\tC4: Full-FT, 2e-5\n86\t  & 0 & 107.81 & 0.0046 &  9.57 & 0.7079 \\\\\n87\t  & 1 & 108.03 & 0.0039 & 10.34 & 0.7107 \\\\\n88\t  & 2 & 108.54 & 0.0040 &  8.82 & 0.7100 \\\\\n89\t\\midrule\n90\tC8: Full-FT, $K{=}16$\n91\t  & 0 & 107.32 & 0.0057 &  8.50 & 0.7057 \\\\\n92\t  & 1 & 106.73 & 0.0050 &  8.02 & 0.7065 \\\\\n93\t  & 2 & 107.11 & 0.0058 &  7.71 & 0.7092 \\\\\n94\t\\midrule\n95\t\\multicolumn{6}{l}{\\textit{Random baselines}} \\\\\n96\t\\midrule\n97\tC5: LoRA, $K{=}4$\n98\t  & 0 & 105.42 & 0.0044 &  9.19 & 0.7005 \\\\\n99\t  & 1 & 108.21 & 0.0067 &  8.76 & 0.7121 \\\\\n100\t  & 2 & 108.72 & 0.0067 & 11.18 & 0.7136 \\\\\n101\t\\midrule\n102\tC6: Full-FT, 5e-6\n103\t  & 0 & 107.17 & 0.0045 &  8.77 & 0.7061 \\\\\n104\t  & 1 & 107.87 & 0.0047 &  8.70 & 0.7094 \\\\\n105\t  & 2 & 108.64 & 0.0065 &  8.82 & 0.7114 \\\\\n106\t\\midrule\n107\tC7: Full-FT, 2e-5\n108\t  & 0 & 108.08 & 0.0046 & 10.97 & 0.7064 \\\\\n109\t  & 1 & 107.85 & 0.0049 & 10.83 & 0.7095 \\\\\n110\t  & 2 & 108.13 & 0.0068 & 11.15 & 0.7094 \\\\\n111\t\\bottomrule\n112\t\\end{tabular}\n113\t\\end{table*}\n114\t\n115\t\\section{ArmoRM Quality Scores}\n116\t\\label{app:quality_scores}\n117\t\n118\tTable~\\ref{tab:quality_scores} reports mean ArmoRM quality scores per generation for all conditions, computed on 200 held-out evaluation prompts.\n119\t\n120\t\\begin{table}[h]\n121\t\\centering\n122\t\\small\n123\t\\caption{Mean ArmoRM quality score per generation. All conditions remain in the 0.110--0.116 range with mild decreases across generations ($-$0.3\\% to $-$1.3\\%), confirming quality stability under iterative self-training.}\n124\t\\label{tab:quality_scores}\n125\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}llcccc@{}}\n126\t\\toprule\n127\t& Config & Gen~0 & Gen~1 & Gen~2 & $\\Delta$ \\\\\n128\t\\midrule\n129\t\\multicolumn{6}{@{}l}{\\textit{Curated}} \\\\\n130\tC1 & LoRA, $K{=}4$ & 0.1133 & 0.1132 & 0.1142 & $+$0.08\\% \\\\\n131\tC2 & LoRA, $K{=}8$ & 0.1146 & 0.1136 & 0.1143 & $-$0.03\\% \\\\\n132\tC3 & Full-FT, 5e-6 & 0.1145 & 0.1141 & 0.1131 & $-$1.2\\% \\\\\n133\tC4 & Full-FT, 2e-5 & 0.1130 & 0.1154 & 0.1124 & $-$0.5\\% \\\\\n134\tC8 & Full-FT, $K{=}16$ & 0.1162 & 0.1162 & 0.1149 & $-$1.1\\% \\\\\n135\t\\midrule\n136\t\\multicolumn{6}{@{}l}{\\textit{Random baseline}} \\\\\n137\tC5 & LoRA, $K{=}4$ & 0.1107 & 0.1099 & 0.1096 & $-$1.0\\% \\\\\n138\tC6 & Full-FT, 5e-6 & 0.1126 & 0.1111 & 0.1117 & $-$0.8\\% \\\\\n139\tC7 & Full-FT, 2e-5 & 0.1112 & 0.1100 & 0.1097 & $-$1.3\\% \\\\\n140\t\\bottomrule\n141\t\\end{tabular}\n142\t\\end{table}\n143\t\n144\t\\section{Lexical Diversity: Self-BLEU and Distinct-$n$}\n145\t\\label{app:lexical_diversity}\n146\t\n147\tTable~\\ref{tab:lexical_diversity} reports Self-BLEU-4 (computed over 500 randomly sampled responses per generation, seed 42) and Distinct-$n$ ($n \\in \\{1,2,3\\}$, computed over all 2{,}000 training responses). Distinct-$n$ is defined as the ratio of unique $n$-grams to total $n$-grams across the response corpus.\n148\t\n149\t\\begin{table*}[t]\n150\t\\centering\n151\t\\small\n152\t\\caption{Self-BLEU-4 and Distinct-$n$ across generations. Self-BLEU-4 remains low ($<$0.016) for all conditions, indicating minimal textual repetition. Distinct-$n$ shows uniform decline across all conditions (curated and random alike), consistent with format homogenization under iterative self-training rather than reward-specific narrowing.}\n153\t\\label{tab:lexical_diversity}\n154\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}llccccc@{}}\n155\t\\toprule\n156\tCondition & Gen & Self-BLEU-4 ($\\downarrow$) & Distinct-1 & Distinct-2 & Distinct-3 \\\\\n157\t\\midrule\n158\t\\multicolumn{6}{l}{\\textit{Curated conditions}} \\\\\n159\t\\midrule\n160\tC1: LoRA, $K{=}4$\n161\t  & 0 & 0.0116 & 0.167 & 0.582 & 0.835 \\\\\n162\t  & 1 & 0.0160 & 0.163 & 0.560 & 0.808 \\\\\n163\t  & 2 & 0.0137 & 0.157 & 0.546 & 0.793 \\\\\n164\t\\midrule\n165\tC2: LoRA, $K{=}8$\n166\t  & 0 & 0.0138 & 0.167 & 0.579 & 0.831 \\\\\n167\t  & 1 & 0.0144 & 0.164 & 0.562 & 0.810 \\\\\n168\t  & 2 & 0.0149 & 0.162 & 0.555 & 0.802 \\\\\n169\t\\midrule\n170\tC3: Full-FT, 5e-6\n171\t  & 0 & 0.0106 & 0.166 & 0.579 & 0.832 \\\\\n172\t  & 1 & 0.0144 & 0.165 & 0.569 & 0.819 \\\\\n173\t  & 2 & 0.0158 & 0.162 & 0.556 & 0.803 \\\\\n174\t\\midrule\n175\tC4: Full-FT, 2e-5\n176\t  & 0 & 0.0131 & 0.168 & 0.580 & 0.833 \\\\\n177\t  & 1 & 0.0117 & 0.162 & 0.562 & 0.811 \\\\\n178\t  & 2 & 0.0101 & 0.160 & 0.557 & 0.807 \\\\\n179\t\\midrule\n180\tC8: Full-FT, $K{=}16$\n181\t  & 0 & 0.0120 & 0.170 & 0.581 & 0.832 \\\\\n182\t  & 1 & 0.0156 & 0.165 & 0.570 & 0.819 \\\\\n183\t  & 2 & 0.0160 & 0.163 & 0.562 & 0.811 \\\\\n184\t\\midrule\n185\t\\multicolumn{6}{l}{\\textit{Random baselines}} \\\\\n186\t\\midrule\n187\tC5: LoRA, $K{=}4$\n188\t  & 0 & 0.0107 & 0.168 & 0.580 & 0.831 \\\\\n189\t  & 1 & 0.0136 & 0.158 & 0.545 & 0.789 \\\\\n190\t  & 2 & 0.0132 & 0.156 & 0.534 & 0.776 \\\\\n191\t\\midrule\n192\tC6: Full-FT, 5e-6\n193\t  & 0 & 0.0133 & 0.169 & 0.582 & 0.833 \\\\\n194\t  & 1 & 0.0133 & 0.165 & 0.563 & 0.811 \\\\\n195\t  & 2 & 0.0121 & 0.162 & 0.550 & 0.795 \\\\\n196\t\\midrule\n197\tC7: Full-FT, 2e-5\n198\t  & 0 & 0.0133 & 0.167 & 0.580 & 0.832 \\\\\n199\t  & 1 & 0.0138 & 0.162 & 0.556 & 0.800 \\\\\n200\t  & 2 & 0.0141 & 0.157 & 0.541 & 0.783 \\\\\n201\t\\bottomrule\n202\t\\end{tabular}\n203\t\\end{table*}\n204\t\n205\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01Vb4jh89bwG62uBhuit2eMG","session_id":"92cca1d5-ce22-4ffe-a33d-e2fb8f40ddd5","uuid":"26ad4ac4-7642-46ee-bfa5-caf091df3cfb","timestamp":"2026-05-17T14:30:52.407Z"}
