{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01MPAmwgBQmjVMk2eTSY4Jeo","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[11pt,a4paper]{article}\n2\t\\usepackage[margin=2.5cm]{geometry}\n3\t\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n4\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n5\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n6\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n7\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n8\t\\usepackage{enumitem}\n9\t\\usepackage{caption}\n10\t\n11\t\\title{The Resilience of Output Diversity Under Iterative\\\\Reward-Based Curation: A Systematic Empirical Study}\n12\t\\author{Anonymous}\n13\t\\date{}\n14\t\n15\t\\begin{document}\n16\t\\maketitle\n17\t\n18\t\\begin{abstract}\n19\tIterative self-consuming training pipelines, where language models are fine-tuned on their own reward-curated outputs, raise concerns about progressive diversity collapse. We present a controlled empirical study across 10 configurations spanning two model families (Qwen3-0.6B, Qwen2.5-1.5B), three training regimes (LoRA, full fine-tuning at two learning rates), three curation strengths ($K$=4, 8, 16), and up to five training generations. We report four findings. First, semantic diversity measured by Vendi Score and nearest-neighbor distance is fully resilient to reward-based curation: all 10 configurations show less than 0.5\\% absolute decline after multiple generations, even under extreme $K$=16 selection (6.25\\% retention). Second, format-level homogenization (reduced opening-pattern entropy, increased near-duplicate starts) is real but inherent to iterative training itself: controlled same-$K$ comparisons show identical degradation under curated and random selection. Third, LoRA fine-tuning creates a capacity artifact that masks curation's true effect; under full fine-tuning with sufficient learning rate, curation mildly \\textit{promotes} diversity rather than suppressing it. Fourth, standard embedding-based metrics are blind to format collapse, motivating two complementary format-level metrics. These findings hold consistently across model scales (0.6B to 1.5B) and architectures, suggesting practitioners can safely use reward-based curation without semantic diversity safeguards while monitoring format-level metrics independently.\n20\t\\end{abstract}\n21\t\n22\t\\section{Introduction}\n23\t\n24\tAlignment of large language models (LLMs) increasingly relies on iterative training pipelines. Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback (RLHF)~\\cite{ouyang2022training}, iterative Direct Preference Optimization, and self-play methods all involve cycles of generation, selection, and retraining. A recurring concern in these pipelines is diversity collapse: the possibility that selective pressure drives the model toward a narrow set of outputs over successive iterations.\n25\t\n26\tThis concern has empirical grounding. Shumailov et al.~\\cite{shumailov2024curse} demonstrated that training on model-generated data leads to progressive quality degradation and mode loss. Alemohammad et al.~\\cite{alemohammad2024self} formalized the dynamics of self-consuming generative models and identified conditions under which diversity contracts. Dohmatob et al.~\\cite{dohmatob2024tale} further characterized collapse dynamics and identified data mixing as a partial mitigation. However, these studies focused on unconditional generation or image models, and their settings differ substantially from supervised fine-tuning with reward-based curation on instruction-following data.\n27\t\n28\tA specific gap in the literature concerns the role of reward-based curation. In typical iterative Supervised Fine-Tuning (SFT) pipelines, a model generates $K$ candidate responses per prompt, a reward model scores them, and the top response is selected for the next round of training. Intuitively, this top-$K$ selection imposes selective pressure that could compound across generations, progressively narrowing the output distribution. Yet no controlled study has isolated whether curation itself causes diversity loss, or whether observed degradation stems from other aspects of iterative training.\n29\t\n30\tWe address this gap with a systematic empirical study spanning 10 experimental configurations. Our study covers two model families (Qwen3-0.6B and Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct), three training regimes (LoRA, full fine-tuning at learning rates 5e-6 and 2e-5), three curation strengths ($K$=4, 8, 16), and both curated and random selection baselines, running up to five training generations. We evaluate both semantic diversity (Vendi Score, mean nearest-neighbor distance, self-BLEU) and format diversity (opening-pattern entropy, near-duplicate start rate).\n31\t\n32\tOur findings challenge the assumed link between reward curation and diversity collapse:\n33\t\\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*,itemsep=2pt]\n34\t    \\item \\textbf{Semantic resilience.} Semantic diversity is fully resilient to reward-based curation across all 10 configurations, including extreme $K$=16 (6.25\\% retention). Vendi Score changes remain below 0.5\\% absolute after multiple generations (Section~\\ref{sec:semantic}).\n35\t    \\item \\textbf{Inherent format collapse.} Format-level homogenization is real but is caused by iterative training itself, not by curation. Same-$K$ comparisons between curated and random selection show identical degradation (Section~\\ref{sec:format}).\n36\t    \\item \\textbf{LoRA capacity artifact.} LoRA fine-tuning creates a confound that makes curation appear harmful to diversity. Under full fine-tuning with sufficient learning rate, curation's effect reverses from suppression to mild promotion (Section~\\ref{sec:lora}).\n37\t    \\item \\textbf{Measurement blind spot.} Standard embedding-based diversity metrics (Vendi Score, self-BLEU) cannot detect format homogenization, motivating two complementary format-level metrics (Section~\\ref{sec:measurement}).\n38\t\\end{enumerate}\n39\t\n40\tThese patterns replicate across model scales (0.6B to 1.5B parameters) and model families (Qwen3, Qwen2.5), providing evidence that these are general properties of iterative self-training rather than model-specific behaviors (Section~\\ref{sec:crossmodel}).\n41\t\n42\t\\section{Related Work}\n43\t\n44\t\\paragraph{Model Collapse in Self-Consuming Models.}\n45\tShumailov et al.~\\cite{shumailov2024curse} showed that recursively training on model outputs leads to irreversible quality degradation and mode collapse, with tails of the original distribution progressively lost. Alemohammad et al.~\\cite{alemohammad2024self} provided a theoretical framework for self-consuming generative models, distinguishing between fully synthetic and mixed training regimes. Dohmatob et al.~\\cite{dohmatob2024tale} further characterized collapse dynamics under scaling laws. These studies primarily examined unconditional generative settings or image models. Our work focuses on conditional text generation under supervised fine-tuning with explicit reward-based selection, a setting closer to practical LLM alignment pipelines.\n46\t\n47\t\\paragraph{Diversity in Language Model Training.}\n48\tMaintaining output diversity during LLM training is an active research topic. Sampling temperature, nucleus sampling~\\cite{holtzman2020curious}, and diverse beam search~\\cite{vijayakumar2018diverse} operate at inference time. Training-time approaches include entropy regularization and KL-constrained policy updates in RLHF~\\cite{ouyang2022training}. However, the interaction between iterative curation and long-horizon diversity trajectories has not been systematically studied. Our contribution fills this gap with a controlled, multi-configuration analysis that isolates the curation variable from confounds introduced by training method, capacity constraints, and iterative dynamics.\n49\t\n50\t\\paragraph{Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning.}\n51\tLow-Rank Adaptation (LoRA)~\\cite{hu2022lora} enables efficient fine-tuning by constraining updates to a low-rank subspace. While LoRA's effect on task performance is well studied, its interaction with output diversity under iterative training has received less attention. We show that LoRA's capacity constraint creates a confound in diversity measurements that can reverse conclusions about curation's effect.\n52\t\n53\t\\paragraph{Diversity Metrics.}\n54\tVendi Score~\\cite{friedman2023vendi} measures diversity through the matrix entropy of a kernel similarity matrix and has been adopted in generative model evaluation. Self-BLEU~\\cite{zhu2018texygen} quantifies pairwise n-gram overlap between generated samples. Both metrics operate on text semantics or n-gram statistics. We show these metrics have a blind spot for format-level homogenization and propose complementary measurements that capture surface-level structural patterns.\n55\t\n56\t\\section{Experimental Setup}\n57\t\n58\t\\subsection{Models and Training}\n59\t\n60\tWe use two base models from different families: \\textbf{Qwen3-0.6B}~\\cite{qwen2025qwen3} and \\textbf{Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct}~\\cite{qwen2024qwen25}. These span a 2.5$\\times$ parameter scale difference and differ in architecture, pretraining data, and instruction-tuning methodology. We employ three training regimes on Qwen3-0.6B: (1) LoRA with rank 64, (2) full fine-tuning with learning rate 5e-6 (FFT-lo), and (3) full fine-tuning with learning rate 2e-5 (FFT-hi). Qwen2.5-1.5B uses LoRA rank 64 only, serving as a cross-architecture validation. All runs use a dataset of 2,200 instruction prompts covering general knowledge, reasoning, and creative writing tasks.\n61\t\n62\t\\subsection{Iterative Self-Training Pipeline}\n63\t\n64\tEach configuration follows the same iterative procedure. At generation $t$: (1) the current model generates $K$ candidate responses per prompt; (2) a reward model scores each candidate; (3) the top-1 response per prompt is selected (\\textit{curated}) or a uniformly random response is selected (\\textit{random} baseline); (4) the model is fine-tuned on the selected data to produce the generation $t$+1 model. Qwen3-0.6B configurations run for 3 generations; Qwen2.5-1.5B configurations run for 5 generations to test longer-horizon dynamics.\n65\t\n66\t\\subsection{Evaluation Metrics}\n67\t\n68\tWe evaluate diversity at two levels.\n69\t\n70\t\\textbf{Semantic diversity} is measured by three metrics: (1) \\textit{Vendi Score}~\\cite{friedman2023vendi}, computed over sentence embeddings of 2,200 generations (one per prompt), which captures distributional diversity via the matrix entropy of a cosine-similarity kernel; (2) \\textit{Mean nearest-neighbor (NN) distance} in embedding space, which captures local separability between generations; and (3) \\textit{Self-BLEU}~\\cite{zhu2018texygen}, which measures n-gram overlap between randomly sampled pairs of generations.\n71\t\n72\t\\textbf{Format diversity} is measured by two metrics we introduce for this study: (1) \\textit{Opening-pattern entropy} ($H$), the Shannon entropy over the distribution of first-token patterns (first 20 tokens) in generated responses, which captures structural diversity of response openings; and (2) \\textit{Near-duplicate start rate} ($D$), the fraction of response pairs sharing more than 80\\% token overlap in their first 30 tokens. These metrics target the surface-level format homogenization that embedding-based metrics miss.\n73\t\n74\t\\subsection{Configuration Summary}\n75\t\n76\tTable~\\ref{tab:configs} lists all 10 experimental configurations. Configurations C1--C8 form a controlled grid on Qwen3-0.6B; C9 and C10 provide cross-architecture validation on Qwen2.5-1.5B.\n77\t\n78\t\\begin{table}[t]\n79\t\\centering\n80\t\\caption{Experimental configurations. $K$: candidates generated per prompt. Sel: selection strategy (curated = top-1 by reward, random = uniform). $G$: number of training generations.}\n81\t\\label{tab:configs}\n82\t\\footnotesize\n83\t\\begin{tabular}{clllccc}\n84\t\\toprule\n85\tID & Model & Training & $K$ & Sel & $G$ \\\\\n86\t\\midrule\n87\tC1 & Qwen3-0.6B & LoRA & 4 & Curated & 3 \\\\\n88\tC2 & Qwen3-0.6B & LoRA & 8 & Curated & 3 \\\\\n89\tC3 & Qwen3-0.6B & FFT-lo & 4 & Curated & 3 \\\\\n90\tC4 & Qwen3-0.6B & FFT-hi & 4 & Curated & 3 \\\\\n91\tC5 & Qwen3-0.6B & LoRA & 4 & Random & 3 \\\\\n92\tC6 & Qwen3-0.6B & FFT-lo & 4 & Random & 3 \\\\\n93\tC7 & Qwen3-0.6B & FFT-hi & 4 & Random & 3 \\\\\n94\tC8 & Qwen3-0.6B & LoRA & 16 & Curated & 3 \\\\\n95\t\\midrule\n96\tC9 & Qwen2.5-1.5B & LoRA & 4 & Curated & 5 \\\\\n97\tC10 & Qwen2.5-1.5B & LoRA & 4 & Random & 5 \\\\\n98\t\\bottomrule\n99\t\\end{tabular}\n100\t\\end{table}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01Vb4jh89bwG62uBhuit2eMG","session_id":"92cca1d5-ce22-4ffe-a33d-e2fb8f40ddd5","uuid":"9f5cc371-73c2-4254-9840-dda6cd5c622f","timestamp":"2026-05-17T14:31:17.067Z"}
