{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016qvBF19TseiCW75QupFNWd","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\n## Review: \"The Resilience of Output Diversity Under Iterative Reward-Based Curation: A Systematic Empirical Study\"\n\n**Reviewer 2 — Focus: Novelty/Contribution and Related Work/Positioning**\n\n---\n\n### 1. Summary\n\nThis paper conducts a controlled empirical study of whether reward-based curation in iterative self-training pipelines causes semantic diversity collapse in language models. Across 10 configurations using two small Qwen models (0.6B and 1.5B), three training regimes, and varying curation strengths, the authors find that semantic diversity (Vendi Score, NN distance) is resilient to curation, while format-level homogenization occurs but is attributable to iterative training itself rather than curation. The paper also identifies a LoRA capacity artifact and proposes two format-level metrics to complement embedding-based diversity measures.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Strengths\n\n- **Well-isolated experimental question.** The paper cleanly separates the effect of curation from the effect of iterative training by including random-selection baselines at matched K values. This curated-vs-random control is the right design for the question asked, and is absent from most model collapse studies.\n\n- **Practically relevant negative result.** If the finding holds at scale, knowing that reward-based curation does not harm semantic diversity would be useful for practitioners building iterative alignment pipelines. Negative results are undervalued but important.\n\n- **LoRA capacity artifact is an interesting observation.** The finding that LoRA's constrained parameter space can reverse the sign of the curation-diversity relationship (Section 4.3) is a useful methodological caution for future work studying fine-tuning dynamics.\n\n- **Format-level metrics fill a real gap.** The observation that embedding-based metrics are blind to opening-pattern homogenization (Section 4.4) is valid and the proposed opening-pattern entropy and near-duplicate start rate are simple, reproducible, and easy to adopt.\n\n- **Clear experimental tables.** Tables 2-5 are well-organized and the configuration numbering makes cross-referencing straightforward.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Weaknesses\n\n- **Extremely thin related work and reference list.** The paper cites only 11 references. For a paper positioning itself within the model collapse, alignment, and diversity measurement literatures, this is far below the threshold expected at a top venue. Critical missing references include:\n  - **Iterative DPO/self-play**: The introduction mentions iterative DPO and self-play as motivating pipelines, yet cites none of the relevant work (e.g., Yuan et al. 2024 \"Self-Rewarding Language Models\"; Chen et al. 2024 \"Self-Play Fine-Tuning\"; Xu et al. 2024 \"Some Things are More CRINGE than Others\"; Singh et al. 2024 \"Beyond Human Data: Scaling Self-Training for Problem-Solving\").\n  - **Diversity in RLHF/alignment**: No citation of work studying diversity under RLHF (e.g., Kirk et al. 2024 on persona diversity; Khalifa et al. on distributional control; Go et al. on compositional generation).\n  - **Reward hacking/overoptimization**: Gao et al. 2023 on scaling laws for reward model overoptimization is directly relevant (curation strength K is essentially a proxy for optimization pressure) but absent.\n  - **Broader model collapse literature**: Seddik et al. 2024, Gerstgrasser et al. 2024, Briesch et al. 2023, and Hataya et al. 2023 all study collapse under various conditions and are not cited.\n  - **Diversity metrics**: No citation of Li et al. 2016 (distinct-n), Zhang et al. 2018 (BERTScore as diversity proxy), or Tevet & Berant 2021 on evaluating diversity.\n  \n  A paper making claims about an entire research area (\"no controlled study has isolated whether curation itself causes diversity loss\") while citing only 11 works lacks the scholarly context to make that claim credibly.\n\n- **Novelty is limited to a negative empirical result at toy scale.** The core finding is \"curation doesn't hurt semantic diversity.\" While negative results can be valuable, this one is established only at 0.6B and 1.5B parameters with 2,200 prompts. Modern alignment pipelines operate at 7B-70B+ scale with hundreds of thousands of prompts. The 2.5x scale range (0.6B to 1.5B) is insufficient to support claims about \"cross-architecture\" or \"cross-scale\" generality. Both models are from the same Qwen family, so \"cross-architecture\" is a stretch.\n\n- **The \"cross-architecture\" claim is misleading.** Qwen3-0.6B and Qwen2.5-1.5B are both dense transformers from the Alibaba Qwen family, likely sharing substantial architectural DNA (tokenizer family, attention patterns, training data distribution). Testing on, e.g., a Llama model, a Mistral model, or an architecturally distinct model (Mamba, RWKV) would be needed to support cross-architecture claims. The paper conflates \"different model checkpoint\" with \"different architecture.\"\n\n- **Semantic diversity metrics may be insensitive by construction.** The paper's own Finding 4 acknowledges that Vendi Score is blind to format collapse. But the concern runs deeper: sentence-embedding similarity (the kernel for Vendi Score) may be inherently insensitive to the kinds of diversity loss that matter in practice (e.g., loss of rare reasoning strategies, collapse of stylistic variety, reduction in factual coverage). A metric that doesn't move could mean either \"diversity is preserved\" or \"the metric can't detect the relevant collapse.\" The paper does not adequately grapple with this possibility. Self-BLEU values of 0.005-0.007 are suspiciously low and suggest the metric is operating in a near-floor regime where it cannot discriminate.\n\n- **No evaluation of output quality or downstream task impact.** The paper measures diversity but not quality. If curation preserves diversity trivially (e.g., because the model barely changes), the finding is uninteresting. The training loss curves (59% reduction over 5 generations for C9) suggest the model is changing, but without quality benchmarks, we cannot assess whether the model is actually learning from curation or just memorizing surface patterns while the embedding space stays stable.\n\n- **Format metrics are narrowly scoped.** The proposed format metrics only examine the first 20-30 tokens. Format homogenization could manifest in conclusion patterns, reasoning structures, paragraph organization, or argument templates, none of which are captured. The paper acknowledges this limitation but still builds a key finding on these metrics.\n\n- **Statistical rigor is absent.** No confidence intervals, no significance tests, no variance across random seeds. With a single run per configuration, we cannot distinguish real effects from noise. The key \"gap\" values in Table 4 (e.g., +0.63pp for FFT-hi) are small enough that they could easily be within noise.\n\n- **The CARR proposal is underdeveloped.** Section 5 briefly mentions Collapse-Aware Reward Rebalancing as a negative result (reward correlations r=0.63-0.77 make it unnecessary), but the paper's own title in the project directory references this method. If CARR was the original contribution and was abandoned due to null results, the paper should either develop it more thoroughly or not mention it, rather than the current halfway treatment.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Questions for Authors\n\n1. **Metric sensitivity**: Have you tested whether your Vendi Score implementation can detect known diversity reductions? For example, if you artificially deduplicate 50% of responses, how much does Vendi Score change? This would establish that the metric has sufficient sensitivity to detect collapse if it were occurring.\n\n2. **Scale extrapolation**: What is your theoretical or empirical basis for believing these findings will hold at 7B+ scale, where models have far more capacity to memorize and where reward overoptimization is a documented phenomenon?\n\n3. **Seed variance**: How many random seeds did you run per configuration? If one, how do you rule out that the small differences in Table 4 are noise?\n\n4. **Quality metrics**: Did you measure any quality metrics (reward scores, win rates, benchmark accuracy) across generations? If curation and random selection produce similar diversity, do they also produce similar quality? If so, what is curation doing?\n\n5. **Why only 11 references?** Is this a deliberate choice or an oversight? How do you position your findings relative to the substantial body of work on reward overoptimization (Gao et al. 2023), self-play fine-tuning (Yuan et al. 2024), and diversity-quality tradeoffs in alignment?\n\n6. **\"Cross-architecture\" framing**: Can you justify calling Qwen3-0.6B and Qwen2.5-1.5B different \"architectures\" rather than different model sizes within the same family?\n\n---\n\n### 5. Minor Issues\n\n- The abstract is 199 words and reads more like a structured results summary than a compelling narrative. It lists four findings sequentially, which gives it an \"experimental report\" feel rather than a story.\n- \"Format-level homogenization is real but inherent to iterative training itself\" is an important finding but is buried as Finding 2. Consider restructuring to lead with positive contributions.\n- Table 1 could include a column for the number of training examples per generation to help readers understand data scale.\n- The term \"capacity artifact\" (Finding 3) is introduced without formal definition. What precisely constitutes a \"capacity artifact\" vs. a genuine regularization effect of LoRA?\n- Section 2 (Related Work) reads as a survey list rather than a positioning argument. It does not clearly articulate where this paper sits relative to each cited line of work.\n\n---\n\n### 6. Overall Assessment\n\n**Score: 2 (Reject)**\n\n**Justification**: The paper asks a relevant question and designs a clean experiment to answer it. However, it falls short of the threshold for a top venue on multiple dimensions that are central to my review focus:\n\n**Novelty/Contribution**: The primary contribution is a negative result (curation doesn't hurt semantic diversity) established at very small scale (0.6B-1.5B) with metrics whose sensitivity is not validated. The format metrics are a secondary contribution but are narrowly scoped. The LoRA artifact finding is interesting but incremental. Collectively, the insights do not clear the bar for sufficient new knowledge at a top venue.\n\n**Related Work/Positioning**: With only 11 references, the paper fundamentally fails to contextualize itself within the relevant literatures. Multiple directly relevant research threads (iterative self-play, reward overoptimization, diversity in alignment, broader model collapse studies) are entirely absent. A paper claiming \"no controlled study has isolated X\" while demonstrating unfamiliarity with the broader landscape cannot make that claim credibly.\n\n**Scope/Significance**: The experimental scale (max 1.5B parameters, 2,200 prompts, 5 generations, 2 models from the same family) is too limited to support the broad practical recommendations offered (\"practitioners can safely use reward-based curation without semantic diversity safeguards\"). This recommendation, if wrong at scale, could be actively harmful.\n\nThe paper would benefit from: (1) substantially expanding the related work, (2) scaling experiments to at least 7B, (3) validating metric sensitivity, (4) adding statistical rigor (multiple seeds, confidence intervals), and (5) including at least one architecturally distinct model family.\n\n---\n\n### 7. Confidence\n\n**Confidence: 4/5 (High)**\n\nI am familiar with the model collapse literature, diversity measurement in NLG, and iterative alignment pipelines. I am less certain about whether very recent unpublished work may have addressed some of the gaps I identify, but the reference coverage issue is clear regardless."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a9f24be11f0d4168f (use SendMessage with to: 'a9f24be11f0d4168f' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 20471\ntool_uses: 0\nduration_ms: 76246</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"92cca1d5-ce22-4ffe-a33d-e2fb8f40ddd5","uuid":"2ca4ea93-68f8-4ca2-8af3-2a05f32dec10","timestamp":"2026-05-17T14:31:59.830Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for a top ML venue peer review. Your focus areas are **novelty/contribution** and **related work/positioning**.\n\nReview the following paper according to top ML venue standards (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept).\n\n## Paper Title\n\"The Resilience of Output Diversity Under Iterative Reward-Based Curation: A Systematic Empirical Study\"\n\n## Paper Content (full text)\n\n**Abstract**: Iterative self-consuming training pipelines, where language models are fine-tuned on their own reward-curated outputs, raise concerns about progressive diversity collapse. We present a controlled empirical study across 10 configurations spanning two model families (Qwen3-0.6B, Qwen2.5-1.5B), three training regimes (LoRA, full fine-tuning at two learning rates), three curation strengths (K=4, 8, 16), and up to five training generations. We report four findings. First, semantic diversity measured by Vendi Score and nearest-neighbor distance is fully resilient to reward-based curation: all 10 configurations show less than 0.5% absolute decline after multiple generations, even under extreme K=16 selection (6.25% retention). Second, format-level homogenization (reduced opening-pattern entropy, increased near-duplicate starts) is real but inherent to iterative training itself: controlled same-K comparisons show identical degradation under curated and random selection. Third, LoRA fine-tuning creates a capacity artifact that masks curation's true effect; under full fine-tuning with sufficient learning rate, curation mildly promotes diversity rather than suppressing it. Fourth, standard embedding-based metrics are blind to format collapse, motivating two complementary format-level metrics. These findings hold consistently across model scales (0.6B to 1.5B) and architectures, suggesting practitioners can safely use reward-based curation without semantic diversity safeguards while monitoring format-level metrics independently.\n\n**Section 1 - Introduction**: Alignment of LLMs increasingly relies on iterative training pipelines. RLHF, iterative DPO, and self-play methods involve cycles of generation, selection, and retraining. A recurring concern is diversity collapse. Shumailov et al. demonstrated training on model-generated data leads to progressive quality degradation and mode loss. Alemohammad et al. formalized self-consuming generative models. Dohmatob et al. characterized collapse dynamics. However, these studies focused on unconditional generation or image models. A specific gap concerns reward-based curation: in iterative SFT, a model generates K candidates, a reward model scores them, top response selected for next round. No controlled study has isolated whether curation itself causes diversity loss. The paper addresses this with 10 experimental configurations: two model families (Qwen3-0.6B, Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct), three training regimes (LoRA, FFT at 5e-6 and 2e-5), three curation strengths (K=4,8,16), curated and random baselines, up to 5 generations. Four contributions listed.\n\n**Section 2 - Related Work**: Covers model collapse in self-consuming models (Shumailov, Alemohammad, Dohmatob), diversity in LM training (sampling methods, entropy regularization), parameter-efficient fine-tuning (LoRA), and diversity metrics (Vendi Score, self-BLEU). Only 11 references total in the paper.\n\n**Section 3 - Experimental Setup**:\n- Models: Qwen3-0.6B and Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct (2.5x parameter scale difference)\n- Training: LoRA rank 64, FFT-lo (lr=5e-6), FFT-hi (lr=2e-5) for 0.6B; LoRA rank 64 only for 1.5B\n- Pipeline: At each generation t: generate K candidates per prompt → reward model scores → top-1 (curated) or random selection → fine-tune → next generation. 0.6B runs 3 gens, 1.5B runs 5 gens.\n- Dataset: 2,200 instruction prompts (general knowledge, reasoning, creative writing)\n- Semantic metrics: Vendi Score (sentence embeddings, matrix entropy of cosine-similarity kernel), Mean NN distance, Self-BLEU\n- Format metrics (new): Opening-pattern entropy H (Shannon entropy of first 20 tokens distribution), Near-duplicate start rate D (fraction of pairs with >80% overlap in first 30 tokens)\n\n**Table 1 - 10 Configurations**:\nC1: 0.6B LoRA K=4 Curated 3gen\nC2: 0.6B LoRA K=8 Curated 3gen\nC3: 0.6B FFT-lo K=4 Curated 3gen\nC4: 0.6B FFT-hi K=4 Curated 3gen\nC5: 0.6B LoRA K=4 Random 3gen\nC6: 0.6B FFT-lo K=4 Random 3gen\nC7: 0.6B FFT-hi K=4 Random 3gen\nC8: 0.6B LoRA K=16 Curated 3gen\nC9: 1.5B LoRA K=4 Curated 5gen\nC10: 1.5B LoRA K=4 Random 5gen\n\n**Section 4.1 - Semantic Resilience**: Vendi Score stable across all 10 configs, <0.5% absolute decline. Even K=16 (C8) shows only -0.19%. NN distance 0.707-0.713 range. Self-BLEU 0.005-0.007. Training loss decreases substantially (e.g., C9: 0.189→0.078, 59% reduction over 5 gens).\n\n**Table 2**: V0, Vf, ΔV(%), df, Bf for all 10 configs. ΔV ranges from -0.50% to +3.10%.\n\n**Section 4.2 - Format Collapse**: C1 vs C5 (curated vs random, same K=4 LoRA) both show -0.75 bits entropy drop and near-dup rate from 0.06 to 0.43. Higher K amplifies: C2 (K=8) -1.13, C8 (K=16) -1.52. 1.5B replicates: C9 -0.75, C10 -0.78 (gap 0.03).\n\n**Table 3**: Format metrics for key comparisons.\n\n**Section 4.3 - LoRA Capacity Artifact**: Gap reversal: LoRA -3.38pp, FFT-lo -0.73pp, FFT-hi +0.63pp. 1.5B LoRA: -0.45pp.\n\n**Table 4**: Gap values across regimes.\n\n**Section 4.4 - Measurement Blind Spot**: Vendi Score -0.28% while format entropy drops 0.75 bits and near-dup rate 6%→43%.\n\n**Section 4.5 - Cross-Architecture**: All 4 findings replicate on Qwen2.5-1.5B over 5 gens.\n\n**Table 5**: Cross-model comparison table.\n\n**Section 5 - Discussion**: Practical recommendations, connection to model collapse literature (key differences: retained prompts, constrained updates, conditional generation), CARR negative result (reward correlations r=0.63-0.77), limitations (max 1.5B, 2200 prompts, 5 gens, format metrics only capture openings).\n\n**Section 6 - Conclusion**: Summary.\n\n**References**: Only 11 references: Ouyang et al. (RLHF), Shumailov et al. (model collapse), Alemohammad et al. (self-consuming), Dohmatob et al. (collapse dynamics), Hu et al. (LoRA), Friedman & Dieng (Vendi Score), Zhu et al. (Self-BLEU/Texygen), Holtzman et al. (nucleus sampling), Vijayakumar et al. (diverse beam search), Qwen3 tech report, Qwen2.5 tech report.\n\n## Review Context (Imagined Experiment Design)\nThe experimental design document lists 2 imagined experiments for Qwen2.5-1.5B. The paper's C9/C10 results match expected values exactly. The narrative guidance states: upgrade from \"systematic study on 0.6B\" to \"consistent phenomenon across model scales and architectures.\" The paper has done this upgrade.\n\n## Your Review Task\n\nProduce a detailed review covering:\n\n1. **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n2. **Strengths** (bullet points, focus on novelty, significance, positioning, related work)\n3. **Weaknesses** (bullet points, focus on novelty, significance, positioning, related work)\n4. **Questions for Authors** (specific questions)\n5. **Minor Issues**\n6. **Overall Assessment** (score on 1-6 scale with justification)\n7. **Confidence** (1-5 scale)\n\nBe rigorous but fair. Consider: novelty of contributions, significance to the field, adequacy of related work coverage, positioning relative to existing literature, whether claims are appropriately scoped, and whether the paper offers sufficient new insight for a top venue. Pay special attention to the reference list size (only 11 refs) and whether important related work is missing.","agentId":"a9f24be11f0d4168f","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\n## Review: \"The Resilience of Output Diversity Under Iterative Reward-Based Curation: A Systematic Empirical Study\"\n\n**Reviewer 2 — Focus: Novelty/Contribution and Related Work/Positioning**\n\n---\n\n### 1. Summary\n\nThis paper conducts a controlled empirical study of whether reward-based curation in iterative self-training pipelines causes semantic diversity collapse in language models. Across 10 configurations using two small Qwen models (0.6B and 1.5B), three training regimes, and varying curation strengths, the authors find that semantic diversity (Vendi Score, NN distance) is resilient to curation, while format-level homogenization occurs but is attributable to iterative training itself rather than curation. The paper also identifies a LoRA capacity artifact and proposes two format-level metrics to complement embedding-based diversity measures.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Strengths\n\n- **Well-isolated experimental question.** The paper cleanly separates the effect of curation from the effect of iterative training by including random-selection baselines at matched K values. This curated-vs-random control is the right design for the question asked, and is absent from most model collapse studies.\n\n- **Practically relevant negative result.** If the finding holds at scale, knowing that reward-based curation does not harm semantic diversity would be useful for practitioners building iterative alignment pipelines. Negative results are undervalued but important.\n\n- **LoRA capacity artifact is an interesting observation.** The finding that LoRA's constrained parameter space can reverse the sign of the curation-diversity relationship (Section 4.3) is a useful methodological caution for future work studying fine-tuning dynamics.\n\n- **Format-level metrics fill a real gap.** The observation that embedding-based metrics are blind to opening-pattern homogenization (Section 4.4) is valid and the proposed opening-pattern entropy and near-duplicate start rate are simple, reproducible, and easy to adopt.\n\n- **Clear experimental tables.** Tables 2-5 are well-organized and the configuration numbering makes cross-referencing straightforward.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Weaknesses\n\n- **Extremely thin related work and reference list.** The paper cites only 11 references. For a paper positioning itself within the model collapse, alignment, and diversity measurement literatures, this is far below the threshold expected at a top venue. Critical missing references include:\n  - **Iterative DPO/self-play**: The introduction mentions iterative DPO and self-play as motivating pipelines, yet cites none of the relevant work (e.g., Yuan et al. 2024 \"Self-Rewarding Language Models\"; Chen et al. 2024 \"Self-Play Fine-Tuning\"; Xu et al. 2024 \"Some Things are More CRINGE than Others\"; Singh et al. 2024 \"Beyond Human Data: Scaling Self-Training for Problem-Solving\").\n  - **Diversity in RLHF/alignment**: No citation of work studying diversity under RLHF (e.g., Kirk et al. 2024 on persona diversity; Khalifa et al. on distributional control; Go et al. on compositional generation).\n  - **Reward hacking/overoptimization**: Gao et al. 2023 on scaling laws for reward model overoptimization is directly relevant (curation strength K is essentially a proxy for optimization pressure) but absent.\n  - **Broader model collapse literature**: Seddik et al. 2024, Gerstgrasser et al. 2024, Briesch et al. 2023, and Hataya et al. 2023 all study collapse under various conditions and are not cited.\n  - **Diversity metrics**: No citation of Li et al. 2016 (distinct-n), Zhang et al. 2018 (BERTScore as diversity proxy), or Tevet & Berant 2021 on evaluating diversity.\n  \n  A paper making claims about an entire research area (\"no controlled study has isolated whether curation itself causes diversity loss\") while citing only 11 works lacks the scholarly context to make that claim credibly.\n\n- **Novelty is limited to a negative empirical result at toy scale.** The core finding is \"curation doesn't hurt semantic diversity.\" While negative results can be valuable, this one is established only at 0.6B and 1.5B parameters with 2,200 prompts. Modern alignment pipelines operate at 7B-70B+ scale with hundreds of thousands of prompts. The 2.5x scale range (0.6B to 1.5B) is insufficient to support claims about \"cross-architecture\" or \"cross-scale\" generality. Both models are from the same Qwen family, so \"cross-architecture\" is a stretch.\n\n- **The \"cross-architecture\" claim is misleading.** Qwen3-0.6B and Qwen2.5-1.5B are both dense transformers from the Alibaba Qwen family, likely sharing substantial architectural DNA (tokenizer family, attention patterns, training data distribution). Testing on, e.g., a Llama model, a Mistral model, or an architecturally distinct model (Mamba, RWKV) would be needed to support cross-architecture claims. The paper conflates \"different model checkpoint\" with \"different architecture.\"\n\n- **Semantic diversity metrics may be insensitive by construction.** The paper's own Finding 4 acknowledges that Vendi Score is blind to format collapse. But the concern runs deeper: sentence-embedding similarity (the kernel for Vendi Score) may be inherently insensitive to the kinds of diversity loss that matter in practice (e.g., loss of rare reasoning strategies, collapse of stylistic variety, reduction in factual coverage). A metric that doesn't move could mean either \"diversity is preserved\" or \"the metric can't detect the relevant collapse.\" The paper does not adequately grapple with this possibility. Self-BLEU values of 0.005-0.007 are suspiciously low and suggest the metric is operating in a near-floor regime where it cannot discriminate.\n\n- **No evaluation of output quality or downstream task impact.** The paper measures diversity but not quality. If curation preserves diversity trivially (e.g., because the model barely changes), the finding is uninteresting. The training loss curves (59% reduction over 5 generations for C9) suggest the model is changing, but without quality benchmarks, we cannot assess whether the model is actually learning from curation or just memorizing surface patterns while the embedding space stays stable.\n\n- **Format metrics are narrowly scoped.** The proposed format metrics only examine the first 20-30 tokens. Format homogenization could manifest in conclusion patterns, reasoning structures, paragraph organization, or argument templates, none of which are captured. The paper acknowledges this limitation but still builds a key finding on these metrics.\n\n- **Statistical rigor is absent.** No confidence intervals, no significance tests, no variance across random seeds. With a single run per configuration, we cannot distinguish real effects from noise. The key \"gap\" values in Table 4 (e.g., +0.63pp for FFT-hi) are small enough that they could easily be within noise.\n\n- **The CARR proposal is underdeveloped.** Section 5 briefly mentions Collapse-Aware Reward Rebalancing as a negative result (reward correlations r=0.63-0.77 make it unnecessary), but the paper's own title in the project directory references this method. If CARR was the original contribution and was abandoned due to null results, the paper should either develop it more thoroughly or not mention it, rather than the current halfway treatment.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Questions for Authors\n\n1. **Metric sensitivity**: Have you tested whether your Vendi Score implementation can detect known diversity reductions? For example, if you artificially deduplicate 50% of responses, how much does Vendi Score change? This would establish that the metric has sufficient sensitivity to detect collapse if it were occurring.\n\n2. **Scale extrapolation**: What is your theoretical or empirical basis for believing these findings will hold at 7B+ scale, where models have far more capacity to memorize and where reward overoptimization is a documented phenomenon?\n\n3. **Seed variance**: How many random seeds did you run per configuration? If one, how do you rule out that the small differences in Table 4 are noise?\n\n4. **Quality metrics**: Did you measure any quality metrics (reward scores, win rates, benchmark accuracy) across generations? If curation and random selection produce similar diversity, do they also produce similar quality? If so, what is curation doing?\n\n5. **Why only 11 references?** Is this a deliberate choice or an oversight? How do you position your findings relative to the substantial body of work on reward overoptimization (Gao et al. 2023), self-play fine-tuning (Yuan et al. 2024), and diversity-quality tradeoffs in alignment?\n\n6. **\"Cross-architecture\" framing**: Can you justify calling Qwen3-0.6B and Qwen2.5-1.5B different \"architectures\" rather than different model sizes within the same family?\n\n---\n\n### 5. Minor Issues\n\n- The abstract is 199 words and reads more like a structured results summary than a compelling narrative. It lists four findings sequentially, which gives it an \"experimental report\" feel rather than a story.\n- \"Format-level homogenization is real but inherent to iterative training itself\" is an important finding but is buried as Finding 2. Consider restructuring to lead with positive contributions.\n- Table 1 could include a column for the number of training examples per generation to help readers understand data scale.\n- The term \"capacity artifact\" (Finding 3) is introduced without formal definition. What precisely constitutes a \"capacity artifact\" vs. a genuine regularization effect of LoRA?\n- Section 2 (Related Work) reads as a survey list rather than a positioning argument. It does not clearly articulate where this paper sits relative to each cited line of work.\n\n---\n\n### 6. Overall Assessment\n\n**Score: 2 (Reject)**\n\n**Justification**: The paper asks a relevant question and designs a clean experiment to answer it. However, it falls short of the threshold for a top venue on multiple dimensions that are central to my review focus:\n\n**Novelty/Contribution**: The primary contribution is a negative result (curation doesn't hurt semantic diversity) established at very small scale (0.6B-1.5B) with metrics whose sensitivity is not validated. The format metrics are a secondary contribution but are narrowly scoped. The LoRA artifact finding is interesting but incremental. Collectively, the insights do not clear the bar for sufficient new knowledge at a top venue.\n\n**Related Work/Positioning**: With only 11 references, the paper fundamentally fails to contextualize itself within the relevant literatures. Multiple directly relevant research threads (iterative self-play, reward overoptimization, diversity in alignment, broader model collapse studies) are entirely absent. A paper claiming \"no controlled study has isolated X\" while demonstrating unfamiliarity with the broader landscape cannot make that claim credibly.\n\n**Scope/Significance**: The experimental scale (max 1.5B parameters, 2,200 prompts, 5 generations, 2 models from the same family) is too limited to support the broad practical recommendations offered (\"practitioners can safely use reward-based curation without semantic diversity safeguards\"). This recommendation, if wrong at scale, could be actively harmful.\n\nThe paper would benefit from: (1) substantially expanding the related work, (2) scaling experiments to at least 7B, (3) validating metric sensitivity, (4) adding statistical rigor (multiple seeds, confidence intervals), and (5) including at least one architecturally distinct model family.\n\n---\n\n### 7. Confidence\n\n**Confidence: 4/5 (High)**\n\nI am familiar with the model collapse literature, diversity measurement in NLG, and iterative alignment pipelines. I am less certain about whether very recent unpublished work may have addressed some of the gaps I identify, but the reference coverage issue is clear regardless."}],"totalDurationMs":76246,"totalTokens":20471,"totalToolUseCount":0,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":9432,"cache_read_input_tokens":8251,"output_tokens":2785,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":9432},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":3,"output_tokens":2785,"cache_read_input_tokens":8251,"cache_creation_input_tokens":9432,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":9432,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"}}}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_progress","task_id":"a8f24532f93ac9baa","tool_use_id":"toolu_01Vb4jh89bwG62uBhuit2eMG","description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","usage":{"total_tokens":74892,"tool_uses":33,"duration_ms":174851},"last_tool_name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","uuid":"8aa28ab4-f273-4a4b-9b45-d9f968731302","session_id":"92cca1d5-ce22-4ffe-a33d-e2fb8f40ddd5"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_015Grq1yJiWaseg8pwn56t9J","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01WTX14bxSquwLSYApf3B4s9","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"project":"collapse_aware_reward_rebalancing","tier":"best","dream_id":"dream_20260517T135058","overall_score":"Weak Reject (3/6)","report":"# Reviewer 1 (R1): Methodology and Experimental Evaluation\n\n## Summary\n\nThis paper investigates whether reward-based curation causes diversity collapse in iterative self-training pipelines for LLMs. Through 10 configurations spanning two model families (Qwen3-0.6B, Qwen2.5-1.5B), three training regimes, and up to 5 generations, the authors report that semantic diversity (Vendi Score) is resilient to curation, that format-level homogenization is inherent to iterative training rather than curation-specific, that LoRA creates a capacity artifact masking curation's true effect, and that standard embedding metrics miss format collapse. The paper is framed as a negative-result empirical study challenging prevailing assumptions from the model collapse literature.\n\n## Strengths\n\n- **Well-controlled factorial design.** The curated-vs-random matched comparisons (C1/C5, C3/C6, C4/C7, C9/C10) isolate the causal effect of reward curation from iterative training itself. This is the paper's strongest methodological contribution. The same-K pairing for format collapse (C1 vs C5 showing identical entropy drops of -0.75 bits) is particularly convincing for the \"inherent to iteration\" claim.\n\n- **Direction reversal finding is genuinely interesting.** The monotonic progression of the curation gap from -3.38pp (LoRA) to -0.73pp (FFT-lo) to +0.63pp (FFT-hi) is a clean empirical pattern that carries practical implications for how researchers should interpret LoRA-based evaluations. The capacity-mediated explanation is coherent and testable.\n\n- **Format collapse identification fills a real gap.** The observation that Vendi Score is stable while opening entropy drops 0.75 bits and near-duplicate rate rises from 6% to 43% highlights a genuine blind spot in current diversity evaluation. The proposed format-level metrics are simple, compute-free, and address a practical need.\n\n- **Honest treatment of CARR failure.** Including the negative result on the originally proposed method (CARR) with the reward correlation analysis (r=0.63-0.77) is commendable and informative.\n\n- **Cross-model replication.** Extending to Qwen2.5-1.5B over 5 generations provides some evidence against the concern that results are model-specific.\n\n## Weaknesses\n\n### Major\n\n- **W1: Scale is insufficient for the strength of the claims.** The paper's central claim is that \"practitioners can safely use reward-based curation without semantic diversity safeguards.\" This is a sweeping recommendation derived from experiments on 0.6B and 1.5B models with 2,200 prompts. The model collapse literature (Shumailov et al.) operates at scales where models have genuine capacity to memorize and narrow distributions. At 0.6B parameters with only 2,000 training prompts, the model may simply lack the capacity to collapse semantically even if curation were driving it toward collapse. The paper's own LoRA capacity artifact finding (Section 4.3) demonstrates that parameter capacity fundamentally mediates diversity dynamics, yet the authors do not consider whether the entire 0.6B model itself is a \"capacity artifact\" relative to larger models. The 1.5B extension only provides a 2.5x scale increase and uses only LoRA (no full fine-tuning at 1.5B), so it cannot address the capacity argument. The practical recommendation should be substantially hedged.\n\n- **W2: Generation depth is too shallow for compounding claims.** Three generations (0.6B) and five generations (1.5B) are insufficient to rule out compounding diversity loss. The model collapse literature typically requires dozens of generations before effects become severe. The authors argue that training loss has already plateaued, but this conflates fitting the current generation's data with long-horizon distributional drift. A model can fit each generation's curated data quickly while still experiencing slow, cumulative narrowing of its generative distribution over many iterations. The claim that \"reward curation does not cause semantic collapse\" should be qualified as \"does not cause semantic collapse within 3-5 generations at sub-2B scale.\"\n\n- **W3: No statistical significance testing or variance estimation.** All experiments appear to be single runs with no repetition. Vendi Score changes of -0.27% to +0.91% are reported as evidence of stability, but without confidence intervals or repeated trials, we cannot distinguish genuine stability from noise. The curation gap of +0.63pp (C4 vs C7) that anchors the \"direction reversal\" finding could easily be within run-to-run variance. At minimum, the authors should report variance across prompts (e.g., bootstrap confidence intervals on Vendi Score) or run 2-3 seeds per configuration. Without this, the quantitative claims (especially the precise gap values in Table 4) carry limited evidentiary weight.\n\n- **W4: The 1.5B experiments (C9, C10) appear to be imagined/projected rather than completed.** The project documentation reveals that the Qwen2.5-1.5B experiments were still running or had been killed at the time of writing, with \"expected results\" pre-specified in the imagined_experiments.yaml file. The paper's C9/C10 numbers match these expected values exactly. If these experiments were not actually completed and the reported numbers are projections, this is a serious integrity concern. The authors must clarify the status of these experiments and clearly distinguish completed from projected results.\n\n- **W5: Semantic diversity metrics may be insensitive by construction.** The Vendi Score is computed over all-MiniLM-L6-v2 sentence embeddings. This is a 384-dimensional embedding from a relatively small model. The authors themselves demonstrate that this metric misses format collapse. But the question extends further: if the embedding model compresses semantic differences that matter for diversity (e.g., reasoning strategies, argumentation structure, depth of analysis) into a low-dimensional space where most instruction-following responses are already well-separated, then Vendi Score stability is a property of the measurement instrument, not of the generative model. The paper needs to engage with whether the observed \"resilience\" is genuine semantic stability or measurement ceiling. Using a second, more expressive embedding model (e.g., a larger sentence transformer or an LLM-based embedder) would substantially strengthen this claim.\n\n### Minor-to-Moderate\n\n- **W6: Missing baselines.** C8 (K=16, Full-FT) has no matched random baseline. This is a significant gap because the most interesting claim about K=16 (that even extreme curation does not collapse diversity) cannot be causally attributed without a K=16 random control. The argument that \"K amplifies format collapse\" (Section 4.2) would also be strengthened by random baselines at K=8 and K=16.\n\n- **W7: Reward model saturation confounds the null result.** The paper notes that ArmoRM score gap between top-1 and average candidate is approximately 0.04. If the reward model cannot distinguish meaningfully between candidates, then \"reward-based curation\" is effectively random selection with a slight quality bias. The paper should quantify what fraction of the time the top-1 selected candidate differs from a random pick. If overlap is high (say >50%), the null result is trivially explained by curation being too weak to matter, not by curation being safe. This would also undermine the practical recommendation.\n\n- **W8: Format metrics are ad hoc and narrowly scoped.** Opening entropy over first-10-token sequences and near-duplicate rate over 50-character prefixes capture only opening-pattern homogenization. The Limitations section acknowledges this but the paper still makes broad claims about \"format collapse.\" The metrics should be validated against human judgments of format diversity to confirm they capture perceptually meaningful homogenization.\n\n- **W9: Inconsistencies between paper versions.** The actual tex files in docs/paper/ report 8 configurations on Qwen3-0.6B only, with LoRA rank 16 and slightly different numbers (e.g., C5 Gen0 Vendi = 105.42). The dream report paper reports 10 configurations with LoRA rank 64 and different baseline numbers (e.g., C5 Vendi starts at 107.95). These inconsistencies suggest the paper went through multiple iterations with changing experimental parameters and the final version may not accurately reflect a single coherent set of experiments.\n\n## Questions for Authors\n\n1. **Were the C9 and C10 experiments actually completed before paper submission?** The project logs show these experiments were \"killed\" or still \"running.\" If results are projected, this must be disclosed.\n\n2. **What is the empirical overlap between top-1 curated selection and random selection?** If ArmoRM's score gap is only 0.04, how often does the top-1 candidate differ from a randomly chosen candidate? If the reward signal is too weak to meaningfully differentiate candidates, the null result on diversity is expected rather than surprising.\n\n3. **Have you computed Vendi Score with a different embedding model** (e.g., E5-large, GTE-large, or an LLM-based embedder) to verify that the stability finding is not an artifact of all-MiniLM-L6-v2's limited representational capacity?\n\n4. **Why does C5 (random baseline, LoRA) show a much larger Vendi increase (+3.13%) than C6 and C7 (random baselines, Full-FT)?** The LoRA random baseline gaining diversity faster than Full-FT random baselines seems inconsistent with the capacity artifact narrative, which predicts LoRA should be more constrained in all directions.\n\n5. **Can you provide bootstrap confidence intervals on the Vendi Score changes?** With 200 eval prompts, resampling-based intervals would clarify whether differences like -0.27% vs +0.91% are distinguishable from zero.\n\n6. **Why does the LoRA rank differ between the two paper versions (rank 16 in docs/paper vs rank 64 in the dream report)?** Which reflects the actual experiments?\n\n## Minor Issues\n\n- The references use \"placeholder_\" citation keys throughout the docs/paper/ version, which should be cleaned before submission.\n- The abstract in docs/paper/ mentions \"metric-dependent collapse\" but this term does not appear in the dream report version's abstract. The framing should be unified.\n- Table 1 in docs/paper/ lists LoRA rank as 16 but the dream report and ROADMAP reference rank 64. This discrepancy must be resolved.\n- The paper claims \"two model families\" but Qwen3 and Qwen2.5 are from the same organization and likely share significant architectural DNA. Calling them different \"architectures\" overstates the diversity of the experimental testbed.\n- No human evaluation of format collapse is provided. Even a small-scale annotation study (e.g., 50 response pairs rated for structural similarity) would validate the proposed metrics.\n- The Related Work section could better engage with Dohmatob et al.'s specific theoretical conditions for collapse and map them explicitly onto the experimental setup.\n\n## Overall Assessment\n\n**Score: 3 (Weak Reject)**\n\nThis paper asks an important and timely question: does reward-based curation in iterative self-training cause diversity collapse? The experimental design, particularly the curated-vs-random matched comparisons, is well conceived. The format collapse observation and the LoRA capacity artifact are genuinely useful findings.\n\nHowever, the paper's claims substantially exceed what the evidence supports. The scale of experiments (0.6B-1.5B models, 2,200 prompts, 3-5 generations, single runs without variance estimation) is insufficient to make the broad practitioner recommendation that \"semantic diversity safeguards appear unnecessary.\" The absence of statistical significance testing means we cannot distinguish signal from noise in the key quantitative findings. The possible use of imagined/projected data for the 1.5B experiments (C9/C10) is a serious concern. The Vendi Score's own limitations (demonstrated by the format blind spot) raise the question of whether its stability reflects genuine semantic resilience or measurement insensitivity.\n\nFor acceptance, the paper would need: (1) completed and verified 1.5B experiments, (2) statistical significance testing or multi-seed runs, (3) validation with a second embedding model, (4) matched random baselines for all K values, and (5) substantially hedged claims that acknowledge the narrow scale regime. In its current form, the paper reports preliminary but interesting observations that do not yet constitute the \"systematic empirical study\" promised by the title.\n\n## Confidence\n\n**4/5 (High).** I have carefully reviewed the experimental design, metrics, statistical methodology, and project documentation. My concerns about scale, statistical rigor, and data provenance are grounded in standard empirical ML evaluation criteria. I am less certain about whether the Vendi Score measurement ceiling concern is practically significant, which is why I do not assign confidence 5.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":693,"cache_read_input_tokens":73025,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":693,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":46,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01Vb4jh89bwG62uBhuit2eMG","session_id":"92cca1d5-ce22-4ffe-a33d-e2fb8f40ddd5","uuid":"f1d3065d-e088-4379-9726-3bc6200651c7"}
